R5.Traditionally, voice and video traffic were sent using UDP, not TCP, because UDP is faster and has lower latency (it doesn’t wait for acknowledgments or retransmissions).
However, in today’s Internet, many voice and video applications actually use TCP, or more commonly, UDP wrapped inside protocols that behave somewhat like TCP (e.g., QUIC).

Firewalls and NATs block UDP more often
Many routers, firewalls, and corporate networks block or throttle UDP traffic for security reasons.
TCP (port 443) — used for HTTPS — is almost always allowed through firewalls.
So, applications use TCP (or QUIC over UDP but on port 443) to ensure connectivity everywhere.

Widespread use of HTTP/HTTPS (which runs over TCP)
Modern apps (like Zoom, Google Meet, YouTube, etc.) often run over the web.
Web browsers and APIs are built to use TCP/HTTPS, making it easier to deliver audio/video that way.


The payload of a TCP segment is the actual data being carried by that segment — the content sent by the application layer (like HTTP, FTP, email, etc.).


